00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:09.950
- [Caitilin] Right, so
what even is a web app?

00:00:09.950 --> 00:00:11.630
Just taking a step back really quickly.

00:00:11.630 --> 00:00:14.880
So, we're gonna be talking
about two big, sort of,

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:17.090
aspects of the code we're doing today.

00:00:17.090 --> 00:00:20.260
So, there's the UI, the user
interface, and the server.

00:00:20.260 --> 00:00:21.980
You can think of the
server as your computer,

00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:24.233
the thing that's doing the computations.

00:00:25.550 --> 00:00:28.220
But everything in the
app is gonna be updating

00:00:28.220 --> 00:00:30.500
based on a user inputting something,

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:32.330
and then there's an interaction there

00:00:32.330 --> 00:00:34.920
with the server to create an output.

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:37.120
So, we'll see what that
looks like in a sec.

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:40.170
So, you can imagine, right,

00:00:40.170 --> 00:00:41.980
how do you guys access
the internet, right?

00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:44.370
Your phone, it can be a desktop computer,

00:00:44.370 --> 00:00:46.363
or a laptop, whatever it is.

00:00:47.650 --> 00:00:50.110
Once you're on there, you're probably,

00:00:50.110 --> 00:00:51.480
I'm sure all of you have experienced

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:53.630
some sort of a web app on
the internet before, right?

00:00:53.630 --> 00:00:56.030
You access something where
you're inputting information

00:00:56.030 --> 00:00:57.960
and something comes out as a result,

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:00.880
and that's all done
via a server somewhere,

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:03.740
so whether it's in your house

00:01:03.740 --> 00:01:05.523
or it's somewhere very far away.

00:01:07.030 --> 00:01:10.150
And so Shiny is a way to sort
of interface between the two,

00:01:10.150 --> 00:01:14.030
the user interface and the server code.

00:01:14.030 --> 00:01:16.560
So, you can think of the server code

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:20.670
as kind of the standard R
script that we've been writing

00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:25.670
R code to do these computations
within our computer,

00:01:26.070 --> 00:01:29.590
and then that's talking
to a user interface,

00:01:29.590 --> 00:01:32.420
and this is where the
HTML comes into play.

00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:36.060
So, we're not really
coding in HTML per se,

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:39.850
we're writing the Shiny
package, the library of Shiny,

00:01:39.850 --> 00:01:42.150
is allowing you to use R

00:01:42.150 --> 00:01:46.160
to kind of communicate
with HTML web interface,

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:47.560
so that way you don't need to, kind of,

00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:48.720
from the ground up,

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:52.823
build your own HTML entirely web app.

00:01:57.930 --> 00:02:02.930
Okay, so, the simplest possible
Shiny app you can write...

00:02:03.161 --> 00:02:05.980
So, one thing, I'll
make sure we all do this

00:02:05.980 --> 00:02:07.670
when I load up R Studio,

00:02:07.670 --> 00:02:09.900
you'll have to install
the package Shiny so that,

00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:12.000
it's the same way we've
installed all our packages in R.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:14.580
So, that's install.packages()

00:02:15.690 --> 00:02:17.217
and then "Shiny".

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:21.290
You'll have to load Shiny itself.

00:02:21.290 --> 00:02:23.360
And then just like I said before,

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:26.080
at bare minimum you need
some sort of user interface,

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:29.070
the UI and some sort of server.

00:02:29.070 --> 00:02:31.170
And so the script you're writing

00:02:31.170 --> 00:02:33.683
in Shiny is gonna define
both of those things.

00:02:35.630 --> 00:02:38.840
And then the last thing that's
happening is the Shiny app

00:02:38.840 --> 00:02:40.360
function creates this app

00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:42.710
building it from however
you made your UI, right?

00:02:42.710 --> 00:02:43.790
UI is here.

00:02:43.790 --> 00:02:46.190
And however you made your
server, server's here.

00:02:47.270 --> 00:02:48.930
And so we're just gonna walk through

00:02:48.930 --> 00:02:50.510
kinda of in broad strokes

00:02:50.510 --> 00:02:53.083
what is going into each
of these components.

